Hi U-Boot Team,

Thanks for all the work you've put into hardening U-Boot's image parsing,
the 2026 squashfs/disk crafted-image series was great to see, and it's what
prompted me to take a closer look in the same spirit.

I'd like to report five independent memory-safety issues, all in the same
threat model as that series: U-Boot parsing an attacker-controlled
filesystem image or partition table from untrusted media
(USB/SD/eMMC/partition, and for some, a netboot-fetched image). I found
them by source review of master v2027.01-rc2 (commit ff8a9bc4), and each
comes with a minimal, self-contained proof-of-concept.

Reachability for all five is an everyday operation on attached media, `ls`,
`load`, `part list`, `cramfsls`, `btrsubvol`, or distro-boot scanning,
with no signature verification anywhere in the path.

Here's the short version of each:

1. squashfs: out-of-bounds free() in symlink path resolution (CWE-590)
   fs/squashfs/sqfs.c : sqfs_clean_base_path()
   A symlink whose target has more ".." components than its path depth
drives
   free(base[i]) with i negative,  a wild free of heap pointers before the
   base_tokens[] array. The `if (bc < 0)` guard only runs after the frees.
   PoC: ASAN heap-buffer-overflow at free(base[-1]). Trigger: /s -> ../x

2. GPT:  integer overflow in partition-entry-array allocation (CWE-190 =E2=
=86=92
125)
   disk/part_efi.c : alloc_read_gpt_entries() / part_print_efi()
   count =3D num_partition_entries * sizeof_partition_entry is computed in
32-bit
   and wraps (even on 64-bit), under-allocating the PTE buffer, while
   part_print_efi() iterates the full unbounded num_partition_entries,
heap OOB
   read + partition-name info leak. PoC: ASAN, 512-byte buffer vs
33.5M-entry loop.

3. GPT: unbounded header_size drives OOB CRC read (CWE-125)
   disk/part_efi.c : validate_gpt_header()
   efi_crc32(gpt_h, header_size) runs on a one-block buffer, but
header_size is an
   attacker-controlled le32 with no bound (Linux rejects header_size >
block_size),
 a multi-GB OOB read before the CRC check. PoC: ASAN read past the 512-byte
block.

4. cramfs:  heap overflow in symlink/data decompression (CWE-787)
   fs/cramfs/uncompress.c : cramfs_uncompress_block() (+
cramfs_uncompress_link)
   avail_out is a fixed 8192, unrelated to the malloc(size+1) destination
sized from
   the inode's declared size; a crafted block inflating beyond `size`
overflows the
   heap buffer with attacker-controlled content. Present since 2003.
   PoC: guard-page SIGSEGV at the allocation boundary. Trigger: cramfsls
over a
   directory with a symlink.

5. btrfs:  heap overflow in subvolume path resolution (CWE-787 / 120)
   fs/btrfs/subvolume.c : get_path_in_subvol() / list_one_subvol()
   strncat(path_ret, tmp, PATH_MAX) accumulates the path across the on-disk
ref
   chain with no total-length bound (strncat's size arg bounds the source,
not the
   dest) and no depth/cycle limit.  PATH_MAX buffer overflow via deep or
cyclic ref
   chains. PoC: ASAN heap-buffer-overflow WRITE once the path crosses
PATH_MAX.
   Trigger: btrsubvol on a crafted image.

I've attached a full advisory for each one with the exact root cause,
reachability, PoC output, and a suggested minimal fix. The PoC sources are
self-contained,  build with `gcc -fsanitize=3Daddress`. I'm also happy to
send the fixes as `git format-patch` against master if that's easier for
you.

Attachments:
  advisory_uboot_squashfs_symlink_oob_free.md  + sqfs_poc.c, evil.sqfs
  advisory_uboot_gpt_oob.md                     + gpt_poc.c, gpt_hdr_poc.c
  advisory_uboot_cramfs_heap_overflow.md        + cramfs_poc.c
  advisory_uboot_btrfs_path_overflow.md         + btrfs_poc.c

I understand U-Boot discloses on the public list by default and that the
project doesn't enter embargoes, so I'm sending this openly. I'll request
CVE IDs from MITRE myself (one per distinct root cause) rather than ask the
project to assign them. I'm happy to send the fixes as a git format-patch
series against master and to help with review, just let me know what's most
useful on your end.

Thanks for your time, and for maintaining U-Boot,
Dilip Mallavarapu

